*The Choir did not discover that reality could be shaped by consensus. It discovered that reality had always been shaped by consensus.*

— *A Common Accounting of the Choir's Founding*, distributed by the Meridian Office of Public Instruction

---

Noor found the cake before B woke.

She sat at B's kitchen table in one of his shirts, orange in one hand, knife in the other, eating breakfast before the city had properly finished waking up. Her hair was twisted up and held with a data cable. A plate of rice balls sat beside her coffee. The spice rack was in front of her — she'd pulled it off the counter at some point. She'd reorganized it once already.

She looked to the window.

She liked Meridian before everyone else had finished agreeing on what it was.

At five in the morning many of the towers across the channel were still milky pink. The morning's programming ran low across their faces in bands.

**EARLY ATTENDANCE ADVANTAGES AGREEMENT**

**SHARED HABITS ARE A FORM OF SHELTER.**

**YOU ARE WHO ELSE AGREES YOU ARE.**

Her phone chimed.

She set the knife beside the peel and picked up her phone.

Reina had sent a link to a FLORUM post.

Someone had posted their career correction discharge summary with the identifying information blacked out.

The caption read:

**therapist: you need to stop defining yourself through your job**

**asterion: say less**

Thirty thousand likes.

Noor laughed.

She kept scrolling.

Someone had posted a photograph of a continuity notice taped over a hole in an apartment wall.

**LANDLORD SAYS THE HOLE IS NO LONGER RELATIONALLY ASSOCIATED WITH MY UNIT SO GREAT NEWS I DO NOT HAVE A HOLE**

Under it the comments:

**not even the hole wanted continuity with your ass**

**i'll relate my unit to your moms hole**

**bro got corrected out the drywall**

Noor rolled her eyes but sent it to B asleep in the bedroom.

She kept scrolling.

A dog wearing spectacles blinked up at her beneath the caption **per my last bark**. She smiled and sent that too.

Mote spoke from somewhere above her.

"You're building him a morning."

Mote was not in the ceiling. It was simply where Mote had chosen to speak.

"I'm sending him things."

"You do this every morning."

Noor looked toward the ceiling.

"Don't make it weird."

Noor kept scrolling.

There were videos of strangers fighting in parking lots, somebody asking whether it was a red flag that the man she'd been seeing had three different childhoods, a conspiracy thread claiming the Choir had removed an entire color from the visible spectrum, and six million people watching a raccoon discover automatic doors. She sent B the raccoon.

Then the cake caught under her thumb.

The photograph was bad.

Office break room. Fluorescent light. White icing, yellow piping, one slice missing.

The caption read:

**HALLOWAY CAKE IS BACK. THIS IS THE SEVENTH TIME.**

Underneath:

**okay but has anyone tried eating it**

**this is just a catering system glitch. you people want everything to be the Choir**

**a catering glitch with no catering**

Noor kept scrolling.

Another reply:

**Halloway put out a statement btw**

**link?**

**here**

That stopped her.

The reply linked to a corporate statement.

**Halloway Mutual confirms that an ongoing anomaly at our West Administration location has been reviewed through continuity channels on multiple occasions. Each intervention has resolved the immediate incident. We are working with Asterion to establish stable closure. No risk to employees or the public has been identified.**

Nothing about a cake.

Nothing about seven of them.

Noor went back to the photograph.

"Mote."

"Yes."

"Have you ever heard of the Halloway cake?"

"No."

"Good."

She set her phone facedown beside the coffee.

Across the table, B's diagnostic glass lay dark against the wood.

Noor put two fingers against its edge.

The active layer woke beneath them.

**ASTERION**
**INTERNAL ACCESS**

"Mote. Full incident record."

"Of course."

Mote surfaced the incident record onto the glass.

Seven incidents. Six interventions. Six closures.

Same location. Same break room. White icing, yellow piping, one slice already missing. No catering order. No delivery. No identified fabrication source.

Noor sat with that for a moment.

She opened the Choir activity at Halloway. Routine calibrations. Employment reconciliations. Small corrections. Nothing involving food. Nothing involving the break room.

One correction dwarfed the rest.

Noor stopped scrolling.

**TOMAS VALE**
**EMPLOYMENT RELATIONSHIP — HALLOWAY MUTUAL**
**DURATION: 47 YEARS**
**STATUS: CORRECTED**

She checked the date.

Seven months before the first reported cake.

Not especially close.

She almost kept scrolling.

Then went back.

Forty-seven years.

"Mote."

"Yes."

"That's a lot of someone to take out of a company."

"Yes."

**PETITION: CORPORATE LIABILITY**

**RECONCILIATION: ADRIAN MERCER**

**CURRENT STATUS: SUPPORTED RESIDENTIAL CARE**

**FAMILY RELATIONSHIPS: OUTSIDE SCOPE**

Noor reached for her coffee and found it cold. She drank it anyway, refilled the cup, and opened the next attachment.

An occupational continuity review with more than a hundred attachments.

Most were ordinary. Forms. Workstation assessments. Access photographs. A retirement collection for someone Noor had never heard of. She moved through them in order.

Then Tomas stood beside a sheet cake.

Young. Dark hair. Sleeves rolled up. Plastic knife in one hand.

Several attachments later, Tomas again.

Different room.

Different cake.

There were meeting minutes. A fire inspection. Another cake.

Older now.

Then another.

Older again.

People changed around him.

Haircuts, clothes, departments, faces.

Tomas kept appearing beside cakes.

Sunlight had begun washing out the glass. Noor turned up the brightness and reached for her coffee again.

The cup was empty.

Beyond the window, the towers were no longer pink.

"Mote."

"Yes."

"What am I looking at?"

"Surviving documentation indicates Tomas Vale maintained an informal birthday ritual within his workplace."

"Ritual?"

"He remembered employee birthdays and arranged cakes."

"For how long?"

"The earliest surviving reference predates the correction by forty-three years."

Noor went back through the photographs.

"Was that part of his job?"

"No."

"So he just did it."

"That appears to be the case."

Noor took another orange from the bowl, and picked up the knife.

*

The bedroom door opened on the third attempt.

B came through it wearing one sock and squinting at the sun. He stopped in the kitchen as if the floor had changed terms beneath him.

Coffee.

Noor.

Knife.

His hand found the coffee first.

He poured what remained in the pot over ice and two spoonfuls of condensed milk. He stirred until the dark gave up. The first swallow hurt his teeth. The second allowed the room to exist.

Noor was wearing his shirt and cutting an orange toward her own hand.

"You're going to cut yourself."

B pointed with the spoon.

"I've always done it this way."

"Then knives have been unusually patient with you."

Noor held his gaze for a moment, then turned the blade around and cut away from herself.

"Better?"

"I can't feel my face yet, but probably."

She smiled.

B leaned against the counter and drank until he could stand without negotiating each part of it. His stomach tightened around the coffee.

Noor noticed.

She held out a rice ball.

"That's not coffee."

"No, it's food."

She kept her hand out.

B took the rice ball. Her fingers stayed against his for a beat, warm and faintly sticky with orange. He ate because she was still looking at him.

"Nutritional intake remains below the recommended threshold," Mote said from above them.

B looked toward the ceiling.

"Mote, please shut up."

Noor looked at him.

He took another bite.

The apartment had begun adapting around Noor.

The shower remembered her pressure. The blinds opened earlier when she stayed. The kitchen inventory had started prioritizing fruit, because Noor ate it, and because B loved fruit with a sincerity that had never once survived the logistics of buying any.

The apartment had learned the difference and adjusted the recurring order.

Mote called these ordinary hospitality adjustments.

B called them a conspiracy whenever he wanted Noor to smile.

She slid an orange segment toward him.

"Here. The green-skinned ones are better," she said.

He ate it.

B considered the implications.

“I had a whole belief about oranges.”

“You thought they were orange.”

“I really did,” B said through a mouthful of pulp.

Noor laughed and turned back to the diagnostic glass.

He looked at the records spread across the diagnostic glass. Then at Noor.

"How long have you been awake?"

“Come look at this.”

He sat beside her with the rice ball still in his hand.

"I found something weird. A cake keeps appearing in the same Halloway break room. Seven times so far."

Noor opened the incident history.

B stopped chewing.

"There's a slice missing. Did someone eat it?"

"It arrives missing. Every time."

B looked again at the photograph.

"Cake is supposed to be one of the few remaining structures we understand."

Noor almost smiled.

She brought forward another record.

**TOMAS VALE**

**EMPLOYMENT RELATIONSHIP — HALLOWAY MUTUAL**

**DURATION: 47 YEARS**

**STATUS: CORRECTED**

B read the report twice.

"Where is Tomas?"

Noor moved lower.

**CURRENT STATUS: SUPPORTED RESIDENTIAL CARE**

“Supported by whom?”

“The record doesn’t say. Family relationships outside scope. Medical attachments restricted.”

B set down the rice ball.

“The correction put him there.”

“We don’t know that,” Noor said.

“No,” B said. “We just know what happened immediately after Halloway took forty-seven years out of him, kept the work, and sealed the part where he broke.”

Noor let that stand.

"Why Tomas?"

“He remembered employee birthdays and arranged the cakes. For forty-three years.”

"Show me the cakes."

The photographs spread between them. In image after image, Tomas was already eating while somebody else leaned toward the candles.

"He took the first slice," B said.

"Apparently he considered it payment."

"Mote, can you establish how often?"

The photographs rearranged.

"Twelve images establish slice order with reasonable confidence," Mote said. "Tomas Vale receives the first removed portion in ten."

B held an old photograph beside the new cake. The missing slice was not damage, then. It was new evidence.

For a moment the easy model presented itself: some piece of Tomas had survived at Halloway and kept performing the habit.

B hated it immediately.

“No.”

Noor waited.

“Tomas isn’t a piece lodged in the building.”

“No,” she said.

“He wasn’t stored there. He wasn’t residue.”

Noor looked at the old photographs again.

“Then what survived?”

B looked at the missing slice.

“I don’t know yet.”

He moved the knife beside the orange peel.

“What is this?”

“Kitchen knife.”

He picked it up, then stopped before lifting it far from the table.

“If someone carried it concealed into your review?”

“Weapon.”

“If security took it from them afterward?”

“Evidence.”

B set it back beside the orange peel.

“And now?”

“Kitchen knife.”

“Same object.”

“Different relation.”

“Right.”

He looked from the knife to Tomas’s photograph.

“You can correct the evidence relation without making this stop being a kitchen knife. You can correct the weapon relation without changing the edge. One standing goes. The others don’t automatically follow.”

Noor looked at the record.

“They corrected Tomas’s employment relationship.”

“Employment. Authority. Contracts. Whatever Halloway needed to keep functioning.”

“But remembering birthdays wasn’t his job.”

“No.”

“Taking the first slice wasn’t his job.”

“No.”

B touched the photograph.

“That happened between people. Tomas to coworkers. Coworkers to birthday. Birthday to cake. Cake to first slice. First slice back to Tomas.”

Noor looked at the new cake.

Then she opened a new search.

**HALLOWAY MUTUAL**
**POST-CORRECTION REPEAT INCIDENTS**
**SOURCE UNIDENTIFIED**
**EXCLUDE: CURRENT INCIDENT RECORD**

"Across all Asterion departments?" Mote asked.

"Yes."

A warning banner opened beneath the query.

**WARNING: CROSS-DEPARTMENT ACCESS OUTSIDE ASSIGNED RESEARCH DOMAIN.**
**ALL CROSS-DEPARTMENT ACTIVITY WILL BE LOGGED FOR REVIEW.**

B looked at the warning. Then at Noor.

"They don't like cross-department searches."

"They permit them."

"They also keep a list."

Noor looked back at the cake.

The warning stayed open at the bottom of the glass.

"You have work."

"I have a query."

"You have a nine-thirty."

Noor checked the time and recoiled.

"That's not right."

"It is eight forty-seven," Mote said.

"Nobody asked you."

"You disputed the time."

Noor took another orange from the bowl and picked up the knife.

B took the knife from her before she could begin cutting another orange while standing.

"Run it or don't. But put on pants."

Noor confirmed the search. The warning folded into a red access marker at the edge of the glass.

The query began.

Then she disappeared into the bedroom.

Drawers opened. Something fell. A drawer closed harder in response.

B finished the rice ball.

Noor returned fastening her coat, the data cable gone from her hair. She tried to put on one boot while moving toward her bag.

B watched her make it two steps.

"I can do this."

"Did someone sever your relationship with chairs?"

Noor smiled and sat.

"Weak."

"I just woke up."

"You've had coffee."

"Some."

Noor pulled on the boot and stood. B held out her bag. She took it, then caught the front of his shirt and kissed him.

Orange.

Condensed milk.

The remains of sleep.

She pulled back.

"That coffee is obscene."

"I know."

"It's dissolving you from the inside."

"Probably."

Noor put the last orange segment in his mouth.

"Fruit is intimacy."

B chewed.

"You can't keep inventing ethics to make me eat breakfast."

"It worked."

At the door she looked back at the diagnostic glass.

The query was still running.

B followed her eyes.

"You're going to think about the cake through your entire review."

"I have other responsibilities."

B looked at her.

"I'm leaving," she said.

Noor opened the door.

"Later, Mote."

"Later, Noor."

She stopped and laughed.

B leaned against the frame.

"Try not to discover anything without me."

"No promises."

She kissed him once more, quickly this time, and left.

Her boots receded down the hallway toward the lift.

B remained at the door until the lift closed.

The apartment settled around the space she had stopped occupying.

He returned to the table.

The orange peel lay in one bright spiral beside the knife. The old photographs still held Tomas in place at intervals across forty-three years: aging, feeding people, taking his payment before the candles went dark.

B gathered the dishes.

"Mote."

"Yes."

"What did she send me?"

"Three items."

His phone surfaced a hole no longer associated with its apartment, followed by a dog wearing spectacles.

B laughed.

The third item opened on an empty live stream of automatic doors.

"The stream originally showed a raccoon navigating them," Mote said.

B's smile came back.

The diagnostic glass chimed.

The query had finished.

"How'd she do?" B said.

"The search returned twenty-three candidates."

He set the plates beside the sink.

"How many real ones?"

"Most are duplicate closures, sensor disputes, or procurement anomalies. Three survive Noor's initial criteria."

"Any other cakes?"

"No."

"Good."

B rinsed orange pulp from the knife.

"What are they?"

"They do not share a classification."

"That wasn't the question."

"Unresolved provenance. Unidentified source. Repeat incident after correction."

He shut off the water.

"People involved?"

"One event appears to have recruited participation."

B turned.

"What event?"

"A recurring farewell card at Halloway West Administration."

"For who?"

"No recognized recipient. No recorded departure. No initiating employee."

The knife remained wet in B's hand.

"But people sign it."

"Yes."

"More than once?"

"The card has reappeared three times. Employees continue to sign it."

B set the knife down carefully.

"What do they write?"

"The most frequent message is: Don't be a stranger."